  * The MIT License                  — see LICENSE-MIT

SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 OR MIT

You may choose either licence; you are not required to comply with both.

WHY BOTH
  Apache-2.0 is the preferred licence and the one new contributions are made
  under. It carries an express PATENT GRANT — real protection for a platform
  that compiles and executes user-authored code — and its section 5 states
  plainly that a contribution is offered under the same terms, so inbound
  contributions need no separate paperwork.

  MIT stays on offer because Apache-2.0 is incompatible with GPLv2, and MIT is
  not. Keeping both means a downstream GPLv2 project can still build on this.

  Neither licence obliges anyone to publish source. That is deliberate: packages
  may ship source-partial, and nothing here turns that into a disclosure duty.

CONTRIBUTIONS
  Unless you state otherwise, any contribution you submit for inclusion is
  licensed as above (Apache-2.0 OR MIT), per Apache-2.0 section 5.
